  "captions": [
    {
      "start": 0.0,
      "end": 4.064541666666667,
      "text": "Two measuring displays disagree about who has the bigger amount.",
      "source_sha256": "31cb204596c78ce6a4e63f1c2c0acf07bae04af97c0b4168a125620d2b991110"
    },
    {
      "start": 4.184541666666667,
      "end": 8.285375,
      "text": "One display has more digits, but does that make its amount greater?",
      "source_sha256": "e3c38602d398430816e0f3d56ec24b2a182d8fcf22f2b94d5dda7fd3356c893c"
    },
    {
      "start": 8.405375,
      "end": 12.831583333333333,
      "text": "Let’s use matching places and a zoomable number line to solve the mystery.",
      "source_sha256": "5e0ceb3047b7b30b92780ab23bf74baa56c4433031756a80d75ec86169f69ebb"
    },
    {
      "start": 13.2,
      "end": 18.077,
      "text": "Today, we’re learning to compare, order and locate decimals through thousandths.",
      "source_sha256": "14acaf47a5efbfe927e26dd556714d98094ff2b636f8d8774fc302dcd6435c41"
    },
    {
      "start": 18.433333333333334,
      "end": 23.954958333333334,
      "text": "You’ll be able to line up places and use end zeros without changing a decimal’s value.",
      "source_sha256": "bfd76f03ebfb723c4672a4083cc774b2ad06d96f22a5e0df54cfc9d573c6c0e4"
    },
    {
      "start": 24.333333333333332,
      "end": 28.54775,
      "text": "You’ll be able to compare and order decimals using place value.",
      "source_sha256": "72f26c1481ca46bcd83b865053e41460891f96787c57890f81d0072ac62578c5"
    },
    {
      "start": 28.9,
      "end": 33.870416666666664,
      "text": "You’ll be able to explain where a decimal belongs between two number-line marks.",
      "source_sha256": "69859ff1aa5b3d036b826d3a3ede9451e19770547ea4170579fb93fa10fc9e05"
    },
    {
      "start": 34.233333333333334,
      "end": 37.589625,
      "text": "Does a longer decimal always mean a bigger number?",
      "source_sha256": "4cbf460a5495fd88050332d89cacea394e087023fcd0f82cb7ee83854436180c"
    },
    {
      "start": 37.709625,
      "end": 38.51958333333334,
      "text": "No.",
      "source_sha256": "e4e8da5869ab7865399dd2b1c7ebc1bceb43c6f73315ffaae94aae9f6c8bb36f"
    },
    {
      "start": 38.639583333333334,
      "end": 40.91458333333333,
      "text": "Compare place values instead.",
      "source_sha256": "c6f39d75a88830a7e8b29e881c5c91881c4599325e5dd5ceb41cd0463eeec918"
    },
    {
      "start": 41.03458333333333,
      "end": 45.754333333333335,
      "text": "Start with the whole number parts, then tenths, hundredths and thousandths.",
      "source_sha256": "67821c5b863591139c6eb82535b248989d798465703a1fca44a08cddd0105e6a"
    },
    {
      "start": 45.87433333333333,
      "end": 51.781416666666665,
      "text": "Adding zeros at the end of a decimal can help line up the places without changing its value.",
      "source_sha256": "bc6f69bcadb477a29c882a045375e9db1e6f5948898733a9ad12069d4087f1d1"
    },
    {
      "start": 52.13333333333333,
      "end": 54.89729166666667,
      "text": "Which display shows the greater amount?",
      "source_sha256": "f4969f8917d9f1b1f34b59d78ceb3611731f9b2c0ada18f2de3d57d0499438bc"
    },
    {
      "start": 55.017291666666665,
      "end": 59.83208333333334,
      "text": "Try matching the decimal places, rather than counting how many digits you see.",
      "source_sha256": "3ff66ef2b5d9ba32d91e8b5208bc5bbd2240aeeb6f7a188fac293319ce5bfb30"
    },
    {
      "start": 61.333333333333336,
      "end": 65.610875,
      "text": "Compare one point four zero seven and one point four seven.",
      "source_sha256": "a74436795ea847dd2f70473939a1f1523a55380c874b204a2823266836a50d4c"
    },
    {
      "start": 65.730875,
      "end": 69.09033333333333,
      "text": "Write the second as one point four seven zero.",
      "source_sha256": "5af301bb30269bc87100db9cb05472ed3b7e438915605fcf6953752c9646a9f8"
    },
    {
      "start": 69.21033333333334,
      "end": 70.98566666666666,
      "text": "Ones and tenths match.",
      "source_sha256": "4cda1f5c191e517db423c100ef3d02620083b6654d700f12b4b65ecf0252d70c"
    },
    {
      "start": 71.10566666666666,
      "end": 74.27625,
      "text": "At the hundredths place, zero is less than seven.",
      "source_sha256": "f69e5e00001de076913df154fa7e25a858962bc9201dc17bd8b0701a06bd90a4"
    },
    {
      "start": 74.39625,
      "end": 79.458375,
      "text": "So one point four zero seven is less than one point four seven zero.",
      "source_sha256": "af7d54ef5f8fb8bb949ee83fc9d81964d4baa51edf81a6f738d69d4b5d2fddb5"
    },
    {
      "start": 79.83333333333333,
      "end": 87.04254166666666,
      "text": "For ordering, line up two point zero five three, two point three zero five, and two point three five zero.",
      "source_sha256": "afe9f2b3e3a0857e7187f3eef36734077983432ad02e8e31f37fa129fef48396"
    },
    {
      "start": 87.16254166666667,
      "end": 91.16054166666666,
      "text": "The first is smallest because its tenths digit is zero.",
      "source_sha256": "72876afb34f94bec66712b2e00ce63a9c3b55d108e1eb99711a1c37696429e31"
    },
    {
      "start": 91.28054166666666,
      "end": 96.28054166666666,
      "text": "The other two match in tenths, but zero hundredths is less than five hundredths.",
      "source_sha256": "5fde1402ae94e02aa2719379899488cb2d736b45451a9d1c8d91b26a5636cac7"
    },
    {
      "start": 96.63333333333334,
      "end": 99.51054166666667,
      "text": "Where might one point four three seven lie?",
      "source_sha256": "27df7b535a13e44019158e13fbf8219996bb32a0f1ebd2db37c32e4910cbaa59"
    },
    {
      "start": 99.63054166666667,
      "end": 102.17733333333334,
      "text": "First find its neighbouring hundredths.",
      "source_sha256": "62abc9dc1cf10039b83ee967a24a1a5d191bedf3b6cbc7e546063cfacedcae33"
    },
    {
      "start": 102.29733333333333,
      "end": 104.52020833333333,
      "text": "Then we can zoom in together.",
      "source_sha256": "d47f85618a2f4a52e8389eb4cc26015dbfb08fdcae6e2fb7ba544ca1e3fcdc1b"
    },
    {
      "start": 106.03333333333333,
      "end": 108.451875,
      "text": "Now locate a number on a line.",
      "source_sha256": "ad083f16fb2822a848c53677daec3af3bd707e1dad61574ed164d0c7e912be48"
    },
    {
      "start": 108.571875,
      "end": 114.84520833333333,
      "text": "From one point four zero to one point five zero, divide the interval into ten equal steps.",
      "source_sha256": "becd810b992c837f62d7e6e2fd38d2681c42b639681f4b8b2b128e1c79e33c2a"
    },
    {
      "start": 114.96520833333334,
      "end": 117.107,
      "text": "Each step is one hundredth.",
      "source_sha256": "a59d695b4971574653d24f892811362ed6b77da08655d69e0b0aae51ffe3c4f2"
    },
    {
      "start": 117.227,
      "end": 122.33216666666667,
      "text": "One point four three and one point four four are neighbouring marks on this line.",
      "source_sha256": "0c5406de3fba855ae8b12bada3d0df3cf132b1daf33a47038366fc3793b521aa"
    },
    {
      "start": 122.7,
      "end": 130.238375,
      "text": "To locate one point four three seven more precisely, zoom in between one point four three and one point four four.",
      "source_sha256": "65658b03202ac3f2f778cd36bd101e033f2027ac08118fb293aa2da2be0daf20"
    },
    {
      "start": 130.358375,
      "end": 133.786875,
      "text": "Divide that interval into ten equal steps.",
      "source_sha256": "05804d3350c22fa58eb9041d302e592550a4f32e74d3d66332923cbb1cb599c1"
    },
    {
      "start": 133.906875,
      "end": 136.19225,
      "text": "Each step is one thousandth.",
      "source_sha256": "979415aa0c73509957e6a6809c746d3ff0b0cc1f448485550ccf07b277d0d329"
    },
    {
      "start": 136.31225,
      "end": 142.02054166666667,
      "text": "Count seven steps from one point four three zero to reach one point four three seven.",
      "source_sha256": "47babf678fab50b489b98aecd8a50dc8501e40cc08d9a988832a3d6c834c52f9"
    },
    {
      "start": 142.4,
      "end": 145.94016666666667,
      "text": "A longer decimal is not automatically larger.",
      "source_sha256": "51212560a5f87cdcf54e5ad9b85e220a1066877334386957fe75a5d2bef85c82"
    },
    {
      "start": 146.06016666666667,
      "end": 150.78475,
      "text": "One point four zero seven is smaller than one point four seven.",
      "source_sha256": "894e5105d6931ec02cabc4499c3973f1deee3034fba2220a0f2f934f9cb6dd19"
    },
    {
      "start": 150.90475,
      "end": 157.382375,
      "text": "Adding an end zero to make one point four seven zero changes its name, but not its value.",
      "source_sha256": "38f337cd2e66d037a6b59a749cdb3b82a547cdbe44b84cc128948519795647ed"
    },
    {
      "start": 157.73333333333332,
      "end": 162.22854166666667,
      "text": "Which is greater: 2.405 or 2.45?",
      "source_sha256": "1391e1b619ae6fca44b773ea239ec5e53afd7ec23c86810d0197253e1c641c23"
    },
    {
      "start": 167.23333333333332,
      "end": 171.01091666666667,
      "text": "Write 2.45 as 2.450.",
      "source_sha256": "3c0c8c78f5c1075db3d3a42e22ceaa84d334a50da2be492be93368c4cbfb58be"
    },
    {
      "start": 171.13091666666668,
      "end": 175.43483333333333,
      "text": "At the hundredths place, five hundredths is greater than zero hundredths.",
      "source_sha256": "feebe3701dc0e7cd40d812cbae91339731efad41f12c17c8ebabb0343e3808b5"
    },
    {
      "start": 175.8,
      "end": 183.338875,
      "text": "Finally, four point seven equals four point seven zero zero: both represent seven tenths after four wholes.",
      "source_sha256": "4d750cb37a64067c95229c8839cb0b8e6b6ed61205ca8f8d775bda680004050d"
    },
    {
      "start": 183.458875,
      "end": 186.521125,
      "text": "Extra end zeros do not add any value.",
      "source_sha256": "7d16767caeb4b0308ab9c370527c35ede33f4eaa4a2314cb1cbffd0d37970450"
    },
    {
      "start": 186.641125,
      "end": 193.61541666666668,
      "text": "Remember: line up the places, compare from the left, and check the equal step size before locating a decimal.",
      "source_sha256": "fc155686e6b16e2ee51fbd77b89b0faa835ff763a2b0694226dc60bc0292ece6"
    },
    {
      "start": 193.96666666666667,
      "end": 198.52266666666668,
      "text": "Our goal was to compare, order and locate decimals through thousandths.",
      "source_sha256": "9fe51d998aa983678671e78ec91e00360909d0d0b098ca0fe957c880ba8fa7ae"
    },
    {
      "start": 198.64266666666666,
      "end": 204.430375,
      "text": "You can now explain your strategy, check the places carefully, and show why your answer makes sense.",
      "source_sha256": "7dae7bccd544f8de6f13545ddba21ec27ec13535ea0882b6adb029853164d66d"
    },
    {
      "start": 204.550375,
      "end": 208.170625,
      "text": "Choose a number of your own and explain it to someone nearby.",
      "source_sha256": "95753b4371494cefb75405559cf20997737c10b2c237dba75c9f74b159d6a809"
    },
    {
      "start": 208.290625,
      "end": 211.478375,
      "text": "Then try the lesson practice on Math Magic.",
      "source_sha256": "5d29954460db2fb9424a6de484decadcdcc1d4bb80e071ff20433c7193077a56"
    }
  ],
